{% comment %}
  The row of style cards under a collection banner - All, Solitaire, Side Stone, Halo ... - that
  lets a shopper move sideways between the styles of the range they are already in.

  WHY THIS IS A SECTION AND NOT BLOCKS IN THE TEMPLATE

  It used to be a `section` of hand-placed group/image/text blocks inside templates/collection.json,
  holding the ten engagement settings written out by name. A JSON template cannot ask which
  collection it is drawing, so every collection on that template - the default one, which is to say
  every collection with no template of its own - carried the engagement strip: Pendants offered
  "Solitaire, Side Stone, Halo, Hidden Halo" and each card took the shopper out of necklaces
  altogether. Wedding's own template had an unedited copy of the same ten.

  So the strip is Liquid now, and decides for itself whether it belongs on the page. It draws on
  Engagement and on Wedding, and on the collections under those two, and on nothing else. A
  necklace, bracelet or earring collection gets no strip at all rather than the wrong one. To give
  those families a strip of their own, add their root and group to `families` below - the lists are
  already in 'menu-static-items' - rather than writing names out again here.

  WHERE THE NAMES COME FROM

  Not from settings. The items are whatever 'menu-static-items' lists for that family's style
  group, which is the same list the mega menu and the mobile drawer draw, so the strip cannot say
  one thing while the menu says another. Rename a style or add one in that snippet and it arrives
  here on its own.

  WHERE THE PICTURES COME FROM

  Our own stock, never anyone else's. Until 2026-11-12 the engagement cards hotlinked eleven
  photographs off miadonna.com, written into this file as URLs: they were the pictures the
  hand-built blocks in templates/collection.json had carried, and they were another shop's
  product photography served from another shop's CDN. They are gone. The chain is now, narrowest
  first:

  1. A photograph in Content > Files named for the card - see the three namings below.
  2. The Image set on the card's own collection in Products > Collections.
  3. The first piece in that collection, which is to say one of our products.
  4. Nothing. The picture box stays, empty, and the name sits beside it.

  So a card shows a ring we sell or it shows no ring at all. A category we have no stock in yet -
  Vintage, Nature Inspired, Toi et Moi and Promise on Engagement - carries its name against an
  empty box until a piece is published into it, at which point the card fills itself with no
  change here.

  The line drawings from 'menu-link-icon' are still available as step 4 instead of the empty box,
  behind "Fall back to a line drawing" in the section's settings. It ships off: a drawn outline is
  not a photograph of a ring, and on a strip where every other card is a real piece it reads as a
  card that did not finish loading. Switch it on for a family whose categories are mostly empty -
  the earring and necklace sub-ranges - where a row of empty boxes would look worse.

  Styles: assets/collection-style-strip.css. Arrows: assets/collection-style-strip.js, a custom
  element rather than an inline script so the row survives a section swap in the editor.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign cs = section.settings

  comment
    Which families carry a strip, one per line as

        Parent | group to match on | root collection | group to show | drawings

    The first two fields are what 'menu-static-items' is asked for when working out whether the
    collection on screen belongs to this family. The third is where the "All" card goes. The
    fourth is the list the cards are actually drawn from, which is usually the same group. The
    fifth is which set of drawings the cards fall back on - "ring-styles", "band-styles", "types",
    "shapes", "stones", or empty for none.

    ORDER MATTERS. A handle is looked for down this list in order and the first family to claim it
    wins, and the lists overlap on purpose: Jewellery > Rings lists Engagement and Wedding Bands,
    because a ring shopper who came in through Jewellery should not have to go back out for them.
    Engagement and Wedding are above it, so those two keep their own strips and Jewellery > Rings
    is left with the collections no other family claims. Men's is last for the same reason - its
    list ends with Bracelets.

    Engagement is listed twice: a shopper on /collections/oval-engagement-rings came in by shape
    rather than by style, and the shape list is the one that has their handle in it. Both entries
    show the styles, because that is the axis the cards browse.

    The drawings are named here rather than taken from 'menu-icon-set', which answers nothing for
    a Jewellery group on purpose: the desktop panel's Jewellery columns carry names
    'icon-jewellery-type' does not draw - the metals, the men's pieces, every ring - so a set
    there would leave half of them with an empty box. The strip's lists are narrower than those
    columns, so it can ask for "types" directly. Check a new family's every name against the key
    lists in 'menu-link-icon' before giving it a set.
  endcomment
  comment
    Double quotes, not single: "Men's" has an apostrophe in it and Liquid has no escape character
    inside a single-quoted string.
  endcomment
  assign families = "Engagement|By Style|engagement-rings|By Style|ring-styles,Engagement|By Shape|engagement-rings|By Style|ring-styles,Wedding|By Style|wedding-bands|By Style|band-styles,Jewellery|Earrings|earrings|Earrings|types,Jewellery|Necklaces|necklaces|Necklaces|types,Jewellery|Bracelets|bracelets|Bracelets|types,Jewellery|Rings|rings|Rings|,Jewellery|Men's|mens-rings|Men's|" | split: ','

  assign current_handle = collection.handle
  assign matched_parent = ''
  assign matched_root = ''
  assign matched_show = ''
  assign matched_icons = ''
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  Extra handles the merchant has named, so a collection that is part of a family but is in neither
  menu list still carries the strip without the lists having to be edited.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign extra_engagement = cs.extra_engagement_handles | replace: ' ', '' | split: ','
  assign extra_wedding = cs.extra_wedding_handles | replace: ' ', '' | split: ','

  if extra_engagement contains current_handle
    assign matched_parent = 'Engagement'
    assign matched_root = 'engagement-rings'
    assign matched_show = 'By Style'
    assign matched_icons = 'ring-styles'
  elsif extra_wedding contains current_handle
    assign matched_parent = 'Wedding'
    assign matched_root = 'wedding-bands'
    assign matched_show = 'By Style'
    assign matched_icons = 'band-styles'
  endif
-%}

{%- if matched_parent == blank and current_handle != blank -%}
  {%- for family in families -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign family_parts = family | split: '|'
      assign family_parent = family_parts[0]
      assign family_group = family_parts[1]
      assign family_root = family_parts[2]
      assign family_show = family_parts[3] | default: family_group
      comment
        A family with no drawings ends in an empty field, which `split` drops, so the fifth part
        comes back nil rather than "" - hence the default rather than a plain assign.
      endcomment
      assign family_icons = family_parts[4] | default: ''
    -%}

    {%- if current_handle == family_root -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign matched_parent = family_parent
        assign matched_root = family_root
        assign matched_show = family_show
        assign matched_icons = family_icons
        break
      -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- capture family_source -%}
      {%- render 'menu-static-items', group: family_group, parent: family_parent -%}
    {%- endcapture -%}

    {%- liquid
      comment
        A group can hand back more than one section ("Heading::items;;Heading::items"); the whole
        family counts for matching, so every section's items are gathered rather than only the
        first one the menus draw.
      endcomment
      assign family_sections = family_source | strip | split: ';;'
      for family_section in family_sections
        assign family_items = family_section | split: '::' | last | split: '~'
        for family_item in family_items
          assign item_target = family_item | split: '|' | last
          if item_target == blank
            continue
          endif
          if item_target == current_handle
            assign matched_parent = family_parent
            assign matched_root = family_root
            assign matched_show = family_show
            assign matched_icons = family_icons
            break
          endif
          comment
            A row that is a URL rather than a collection - a filter link such as
            /collections/necklaces?filter.p.tag=style:tennis - still names a collection in its path,
            and a shopper standing on that collection is in the family.
          endcomment
          assign first_character = item_target | slice: 0, 1
          if first_character == '/' and item_target contains '/collections/'
            assign path_handle = item_target | split: '/collections/' | last | split: '?' | first
            if path_handle == current_handle
              assign matched_parent = family_parent
              assign matched_root = family_root
              assign matched_show = family_show
              assign matched_icons = family_icons
              break
            endif
          endif
        endfor
        if matched_parent != blank
          break
        endif
      endfor
      if matched_parent != blank
        break
      endif
    -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- liquid
  comment
    Which of the family's lists the cards are drawn from, and which drawings they fall back on:
    both were settled when the family claimed the collection.
  endcomment
  assign show_group = matched_show
  assign icon_set = matched_icons
  assign root_collection = collections[matched_root]
-%}

{%- capture strip_source -%}
  {%- if matched_parent != blank -%}
    {%- render 'menu-static-items', group: show_group, parent: matched_parent -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- liquid
  comment
    Only the first section of the group is drawn, the same rule the desktop panel follows: a second
    would need a heading of its own and the strip has nowhere to put one.
  endcomment
  assign strip_items = strip_source | strip | split: ';;' | first | split: '::' | last | split: '~'
  assign has_items = false
  if matched_parent != blank and strip_items.size > 0
    assign has_items = true
  endif
-%}

{%- if has_items -%}
  {{ 'collection-style-strip.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
  <script src="{{ 'collection-style-strip.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>

  {%- liquid
    assign all_label = cs.all_label | default: 'All'
    assign all_url = cs.all_link
    if all_url == blank
      assign all_url = root_collection.url | default: routes.all_products_collection_url
    endif

    assign card_border = cs.card_border_color
    assign label_case = cs.label_transform | default: 'uppercase'
  -%}

  {%- style -%}
    #style-strip-{{ section.id }} {
      display: block;
      --strip-gap: {{ cs.gap }}px;
      --strip-per-row: {{ cs.per_row }};
      --strip-per-row-mobile: {{ cs.per_row_mobile }};
      --strip-card-height: {{ cs.card_height }}px;
      --strip-media-width: {{ cs.media_width }}px;
      --strip-media-width-mobile: {{ cs.media_width_mobile }}px;
      --strip-icon-size: {{ cs.icon_size }}px;
      --strip-label-size: {{ cs.label_size }}px;
      --strip-label-size-mobile: {{ cs.label_size_mobile }}px;
      --strip-label-tracking: {{ cs.label_tracking | divided_by: 100.0 }}em;
      --strip-label-case: {{ label_case }};
      --strip-border-width: {{ cs.card_border_width }}px;
      padding-block: {{ cs.padding_top }}px {{ cs.padding_bottom }}px;
      {%- if cs.section_background != blank and cs.section_background != 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' %}
      background-color: {{ cs.section_background }};
      {%- endif %}
      {%- if cs.card_background != blank and cs.card_background != 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' %}
      --strip-card-bg: {{ cs.card_background }};
      {%- endif %}
      {%- if cs.media_background != blank and cs.media_background != 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' %}
      --strip-media-bg: {{ cs.media_background }};
      {%- endif %}
      {%- if card_border != blank and card_border != 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' %}
      --strip-card-border: {{ card_border }};
      {%- endif %}
      {%- if cs.label_color != blank and cs.label_color != 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' %}
      --strip-label-color: {{ cs.label_color }};
      {%- endif %}
      {%- if cs.active_border_color != blank and cs.active_border_color != 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' %}
      --strip-active-border: {{ cs.active_border_color }};
      {%- endif %}
      {%- if cs.active_background != blank and cs.active_background != 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' %}
      --strip-active-bg: {{ cs.active_background }};
      {%- endif %}
      {%- if cs.arrow_color != blank and cs.arrow_color != 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' %}
      --strip-arrow-color: {{ cs.arrow_color }};
      {%- endif %}
      {%- if cs.heading_color != blank and cs.heading_color != 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' %}
      --strip-heading-color: {{ cs.heading_color }};
      {%- endif %}
      --strip-heading-size: {{ cs.heading_size }}px;
      --strip-heading-size-mobile: {{ cs.heading_size_mobile }}px;
    }
  {%- endstyle -%}

  <collection-style-strip
    id="style-strip-{{ section.id }}"
    class="style-strip{% unless cs.show_arrows_mobile %} style-strip--no-arrows-mobile{% endunless %}"
  >
    <div class="style-strip__inner{% if cs.width == 'page' %} style-strip__inner--page{% endif %}">
      {%- if cs.heading != blank or cs.show_arrows -%}
        <div class="style-strip__head{% if cs.heading == blank %} style-strip__head--bare{% endif %}">
          {%- if cs.heading != blank -%}
            <h2 class="style-strip__heading">{{ cs.heading }}</h2>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- if cs.show_arrows -%}
            <div class="style-strip__arrows" data-strip-arrows>
              <button
                type="button"
                class="style-strip__arrow style-strip__arrow--prev"
                aria-label="Previous"
              >
                {%- render 'icon-chevron-left' -%}
              </button>
              <button
                type="button"
                class="style-strip__arrow style-strip__arrow--next"
                aria-label="Next"
              >
                {%- render 'icon-chevron-left' -%}
              </button>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}

      <ul class="style-strip__track" role="list" data-strip-track>
        {%- if cs.show_all_card -%}
          {%- liquid
            comment
              The "All" card has no name in any icon set to fall back on, so it works down the
              same chain the cards below do: the picture the merchant chose, then All.png in
              Files, then `category-<range>.png`, then the range's own collection image, then the
              first piece in the range. The last of those is the one that answers in practice -
              Engagement Rings holds stock and has no Image set - so "All" shows one of our rings.
              With none of the five the box stays empty.
            endcomment
            assign all_photo = cs.all_image
            if all_photo == blank
              assign all_photo = images['All.png']
            endif
            comment
              The same `category-<handle>.png` naming the cards below use, on the range's own
              collection - `category-earrings.png` gives Earrings an "All" picture without
              anything being set in the admin.
            endcomment
            if all_photo == blank
              assign all_category_photo_name = matched_root | prepend: 'category-' | append: '.png'
              assign all_photo = images[all_category_photo_name]
            endif
            if all_photo == blank
              assign all_photo = root_collection.image
            endif
            if all_photo == blank
              assign all_photo = root_collection.products.first.featured_image
            endif
          -%}
          <li class="style-strip__item">
            <a
              class="style-strip__card{% if current_handle == matched_root %} style-strip__card--active{% endif %}{% if all_photo == blank %} style-strip__card--empty{% endif %}"
              href="{{ all_url }}"
              {% if current_handle == matched_root %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
            >
              <span class="style-strip__media">
                {%- if all_photo != blank -%}
                  <img
                    src="{{ all_photo | image_url: width: 240 }}"
                    srcset="{{ all_photo | image_url: width: 240 }} 240w, {{ all_photo | image_url: width: 480 }} 480w"
                    sizes="{{ cs.media_width }}px"
                    alt=""
                    width="240"
                    height="240"
                    loading="lazy"
                  >
                {%- endif -%}
              </span>
              <span class="style-strip__label">{{ all_label }}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- for strip_item in strip_items -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign item_parts = strip_item | split: '|'
            assign item_label = item_parts[0] | strip
            assign item_target = item_parts | last | strip
            if item_label == blank or item_target == blank
              continue
            endif

            comment
              A target that starts with "/" is a URL written out in full - a page, or a collection
              with a filter on it - and is used exactly as it stands. Anything else is a collection
              handle.
            endcomment
            assign item_first_character = item_target | slice: 0, 1
            assign item_handle = ''
            assign item_is_url = false
            if item_first_character == '/'
              comment
                A row written as a URL is a sub-range the shop has no collection for yet, filtered
                out of its family's collection - Tennis is /collections/necklaces with the
                `style:tennis` tag on it. It is NOT that collection: the card is one of several
                pointing into the same place, so it never counts as the card you are on, and it
                never borrows that collection's picture - four cards carrying one photograph would
                read as a fault. 'menu-static-items' explains why the rows are written this way
                and says to swap each one for a collection as soon as that collection exists.
              endcomment
              assign item_is_url = true
              assign item_url = item_target
            else
              assign item_handle = item_target
              assign item_collection = collections[item_target]
              comment
                A handle the shop has no collection for still gets its ordinary /collections/ URL:
                the 404 template hands that path to 'empty-category', which heads it with the
                category's name rather than saying the page does not exist.
              endcomment
              if item_collection.handle == item_target
                assign item_url = item_collection.url
              else
                assign item_url = item_target | prepend: '/collections/'
              endif
            endif

            comment
              The drawing is keyed on the item's own name, never on its collection: "Round" is
              `round` under both Engagement and Diamonds, where the collections are
              'round-engagement-rings' and 'round-diamonds'.
            endcomment
            assign item_key = item_label | handle
            comment
              Three names are tried in Content > Files, narrowest first.

              1. `category-<family>-<handle>.png`. Two families can give a card the same name -
                 Tennis is a necklace under Necklaces and a bracelet under Bracelets - and a name
                 on its own cannot tell them apart, so a picture meant for one of them is filed
                 against its family as well: `category-bracelets-tennis.png`.
              2. The label with its spaces turned into underscores, `Side_Stone.png`. This is what
                 the hand-built strip used and where the necklace pictures are filed.
              3. `category-<handle>.png`, the naming the menu tiles already use
                 ('header-menu-drawer-links' asks for `category-best-sellers.png`). Reach for this
                 one whenever a label carries punctuation: Shopify rewrites a file name on upload,
                 so "Climbers, Jackets & Cuffs" can never be relied on to come back as
                 `Climbers,_Jackets_&_Cuffs.png`, while `category-climbers-jackets-cuffs.png` is
                 safe in both directions.
            endcomment
            assign item_family_photo_name = matched_root | append: '-' | append: item_key | prepend: 'category-' | append: '.png'
            assign item_photo = images[item_family_photo_name]
            if item_photo == blank
              assign item_photo_name = item_label | replace: ' ', '_' | append: '.png'
              assign item_photo = images[item_photo_name]
            endif
            if item_photo == blank
              assign item_category_photo_name = item_key | prepend: 'category-' | append: '.png'
              assign item_photo = images[item_category_photo_name]
            endif

            comment
              No photograph filed for the card, so the collection it opens stands in for itself:
              the Image set on it in Products > Collections first, then the first piece in it,
              which is one of our own products. This is what carries the strip - none of the
              style collections has an Image, so every card that has anything to show is showing
              a ring we sell.

              A category with no stock shows nothing: Vintage, Nature Inspired, Toi et Moi and
              Promise under Engagement, Plain Metal and Ready to Ship under Wedding, and most of
              the earring and necklace sub-ranges. Publish a piece into one - or set an Image on
              it - and that card fills itself with nothing to change here.

              A card that is a filter URL rather than a collection is skipped: several of them
              point into one collection, so they would all come back carrying the same picture.
            endcomment
            if item_photo == blank and item_is_url == false
              assign item_photo = item_collection.image
              if item_photo == blank
                assign item_photo = item_collection.products.first.featured_image
              endif
            endif
          -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            The line drawing, which is only reached when the setting asks for it. Rendering it
            unconditionally and then throwing it away would still cost the render.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- capture item_icon -%}
            {%- if cs.show_drawings and item_photo == blank -%}
              {%- render 'menu-link-icon',
                icons: icon_set,
                key: item_key,
                prefix: 'style-strip__icon',
                source_width: 240
              -%}
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endcapture -%}

          {%- liquid
            assign item_icon = item_icon | strip

            assign is_current = false
            if item_is_url == false and item_handle == current_handle
              assign is_current = true
            endif

            comment
              Wedding's style list opens with "Wedding", which is the wedding bands collection
              itself - the same place the "All" card goes. Two cards side by side pointing at one
              collection, both lit up as the card you are on, reads as a fault, so the list's own
              row stands down when the "All" card is covering it.
            endcomment
            if cs.show_all_card and item_is_url == false and item_handle == matched_root
              continue
            endif
          -%}

          <li class="style-strip__item">
            <a
              class="style-strip__card{% if is_current %} style-strip__card--active{% endif %}{% if item_photo == blank and item_icon != blank %} style-strip__card--drawn{% endif %}{% if item_photo == blank and item_icon == blank %} style-strip__card--empty{% endif %}"
              href="{{ item_url }}"
              {% if is_current %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
            >
              {%- comment -%}
                Nothing to show: the box stays, so the names stay in a line down the row and the
                card is the same shape as the one beside it, but it is left empty and untinted.
                A tinted square with nothing in it reads as a photograph that failed to load.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <span class="style-strip__media">
                {%- if item_photo != blank -%}
                  <img
                    src="{{ item_photo | image_url: width: 240 }}"
                    srcset="{{ item_photo | image_url: width: 240 }} 240w, {{ item_photo | image_url: width: 480 }} 480w"
                    sizes="{{ cs.media_width }}px"
                    alt=""
                    width="240"
                    height="240"
                    loading="lazy"
                  >
                {%- else -%}
                  {{ item_icon }}
                {%- endif -%}
              </span>
              <span class="style-strip__label">{{ item_label }}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </collection-style-strip>
{%- elsif request.design_mode -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Nothing is drawn on a collection outside the two families, which is the whole point of the
    section; in the editor that would leave an unselectable blank, so it says why instead. Shoppers
    never see this.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="style-strip__design-note">
    Style cards are hidden here. This row only appears on Engagement and Wedding collections and
    the collections under them. On {{ collection.title | default: 'this page' }} it stays out of
    the way.
  </div>
  <style>
    .style-strip__design-note {
      margin: 0 auto;
      max-width: 60rem;
      padding: 2.4rem;
      border: 1px dashed rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25);
      font-size: 1.3rem;
      line-height: 1.5;
      text-align: center;
    }
  </style>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Collection style cards",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "section section-collection-style-strip",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "A row of style cards for browsing sideways within a range. It draws on Engagement and Wedding collections and the collections under them, and hides itself everywhere else, so one default template can carry it without a necklace or earring collection offering ring settings. The names come from the mega menu lists, so the two can never disagree."
    },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Heading" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "info": "Leave empty for no heading - the cards then sit straight under the banner, which is how the row shipped." },
    { "type": "range", "id": "heading_size", "label": "Heading size", "min": 12, "max": 48, "step": 1, "unit": "px", "default": 18 },
    { "type": "range", "id": "heading_size_mobile", "label": "Heading size on mobile", "min": 10, "max": 36, "step": 1, "unit": "px", "default": 15 },
    { "type": "color", "id": "heading_color", "label": "Heading colour", "default": "rgba(0,0,0,0)", "info": "Leave empty to use the page's own text colour." },
    { "type": "header", "content": "The \"All\" card" },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_all_card", "label": "Show an \"All\" card first", "default": true },
    { "type": "text", "id": "all_label", "label": "\"All\" card label", "default": "All" },
    { "type": "url", "id": "all_link", "label": "\"All\" card link", "info": "Leave empty to open the range's own collection - Engagement Rings or Wedding Bands, whichever family the page belongs to." },
    { "type": "image_picker", "id": "all_image", "label": "\"All\" card picture", "info": "Leave empty to use All.png from Content > Files, then the range's own collection image, then the first piece in the range." },
